xinput: a Sony badge is not a Sony descriptor.

mgvf-0012 taught xinput that a gamepad's axes and buttons come in two
conventions rather than one, and decided which a pad follows by reading its
vendor id. 0x054c meant the Sony order: right stick on Z and Rz, triggers on Rx
and Ry.

That was right for as long as a Sony pad reached xinput carrying its own report
descriptor, which is every pad on the raw route. It stops being right the moment
the pad goes through winebus's SDL backend, because wine then throws the pad's
descriptor away and builds an Xbox-shaped one in its place -- and the vendor id
stays 0x054c, because it is still the same physical pad on the same bus.

So xinput applied the Sony order to a descriptor that was already in Xbox order,
and the two corrections cancelled into a fault: God of War Ragnarok on that
route read its right stick off the trigger axes. That is this project's own
regression and it has been open since mgvf-0012 shipped.

ASK THE DESCRIPTOR. The X axis is the signal, and it is not a heuristic dressed
up as one:

  a Sony pad's own descriptor    X is an unsigned byte, logical 0..255
  wine's synthetic gamepad       X is signed 16-bit, logical minimum negative

One comparison separates them, and it is read from the very descriptor whose
order is in question rather than from a badge that survives the descriptor being
replaced. The vendor check stays in front of it: a pad that is not Sony was
never going to be given this order, and asking its axes about it would only be a
second way to get the same answer.

A pad that declares no X axis at all falls to the Xbox order with a WARN, which
is what every build before mgvf-0012 gave everything.

AND THE TRACE SAYS WHICH AND WHY, with the logical range in it. "Uses the Sony
axis and button order" on its own could not distinguish a correct decision from
this defect, which is part of why the defect stood as long as it did.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: make God of War Ragnarok work through SDL. It stops this
driver from breaking it there. That title wants Steam Input, and with Steam
Input on it is Steam's virtual pad the game reads, not ours; on the raw route,
with the pad's own descriptor, the glyphs and the axes were already right. The
fix here is a revert of our own damage, not a feature.

ONE BINARY SERVES BOTH ROUTES, which is the whole point of reading the
descriptor rather than adding an option: the raw route still gets the Sony
order, the SDL route stops getting it, and nobody has to know which route their
pad took.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
---
--- a/dlls/xinput1_3/main.c
+++ b/dlls/xinput1_3/main.c
@@ -261,14 +261,45 @@
 
     /* MGVF mgvf-0012: which of the two axis conventions this pad follows.
      * 0x054c is Sony: every DualShock 4, DualSense and DualSense Edge puts the
-     * right stick on Z/Rz and the triggers on Rx/Ry. */
+     * right stick on Z/Rz and the triggers on Rx/Ry.
+     *
+     * MGVF mgvf-0027: AND THE VENDOR ALONE IS NOT THE QUESTION. It was, for as
+     * long as a Sony pad reached this code carrying its own report descriptor.
+     * It stops being one the moment the pad goes through winebus's SDL backend,
+     * because wine then throws the pad's descriptor away and synthesises an
+     * Xbox-shaped one for it -- while the vendor id stays 0x054c, since it is
+     * still the same physical pad. The Sony layout was then applied to a
+     * descriptor that was already in Xbox order, and both were undone: God of
+     * War Ragnarok on that route read its right stick off the triggers.
+     *
+     * So the descriptor is asked, not the badge. The X axis is the signal and
+     * it is not a heuristic: a Sony pad declares it as an unsigned byte, 0 to
+     * 255, and wine's synthetic gamepad declares a signed 16-bit axis whose
+     * logical minimum is negative. One comparison separates them, it is read
+     * from the very descriptor whose order is in question, and a pad that
+     * declares no X axis at all gets the Xbox order -- which is what every
+     * build before mgvf-0012 gave everything. */
     {
         HIDD_ATTRIBUTES attrs = {sizeof(attrs)};
+        HIDP_VALUE_CAPS x_caps;
+        USHORT x_count = 1;
+
         if (HidD_GetAttributes(device, &attrs) && attrs.VendorID == 0x054c)
         {
-            TRACE("%04x:%04x uses the Sony axis and button order\n",
-                  attrs.VendorID, attrs.ProductID);
-            controller->hid.sony_layout = TRUE;
+            if (HidP_GetSpecificValueCaps(HidP_Input, HID_USAGE_PAGE_GENERIC, 0, HID_USAGE_GENERIC_X,
+                                          &x_caps, &x_count, preparsed) != HIDP_STATUS_SUCCESS || !x_count)
+                WARN("%04x:%04x declares no X axis; using the Xbox axis and button order\n",
+                     attrs.VendorID, attrs.ProductID);
+            else if (x_caps.LogicalMin < 0)
+                TRACE("%04x:%04x is a Sony pad whose X axis is signed (%ld..%ld), so this descriptor is not"
+                      " the pad's own -- using the Xbox axis and button order\n",
+                      attrs.VendorID, attrs.ProductID, (long)x_caps.LogicalMin, (long)x_caps.LogicalMax);
+            else
+            {
+                TRACE("%04x:%04x uses the Sony axis and button order (X is %ld..%ld, the pad's own descriptor)\n",
+                      attrs.VendorID, attrs.ProductID, (long)x_caps.LogicalMin, (long)x_caps.LogicalMax);
+                controller->hid.sony_layout = TRUE;
+            }
         }
     }
 
